{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## TRIAGE-2E47-A: three of four triaged and landed for review (Gerrit 1375); the fourth stays open\n\n### 1. `IDEA` does not exist — NOT A DEFECT. Disposition: change nothing.\n\nThe finding is accurate about the instance and wrong about rebar. `idea ↔ IDEA` is a deliberate\n**injective** mapping whose whole purpose is to round-trip WITHOUT a `rebar-status:` annotation\nlabel (unlike `blocked`/`cancelled`, which need one because the forward map is non-injective). It\ncarries a documented **operator prerequisite**: `config.py`'s own note at the mapping, and\n`docs/jira-sync-setup.md` §\"The `idea` status ↔ Jira `IDEA`\", which spells out the required status\nplus the transitions into and out of it, and states that without them an outbound `IDEA` mutation\nfails.\n\nSo the harness instance — a stock Scrum template — simply has not met a prerequisite rebar already\ndocuments. The tempting fix, remapping `idea → \"To Do\"`, would **break the documented round-trip on\nevery instance that HAS provisioned `IDEA`** in order to accommodate one that has not. Pinned as\nrefused, with the reasoning in the test, so the next reader does not re-litigate it from scratch.\n\n### 2. Label limit 254 vs 255 — CONFIRMED, and the obvious fix is a Cloud REGRESSION\n\nI made this mistake and the epic's own oracles caught it. Tightening the shared\n`JIRA_LABEL_MAX_CHARS` to 254 turned **four characterization oracles red**, which is exactly what\nthey exist for:\n\n    tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_backend_characterization.py::test_sanitize_label_at_inclusive_limit_passes\n    tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_dc_backend_characterization.py::test_dc_sanitize_label_pins_the_shared_token_rules\n    tests/scripts/bridge/.../test_sanitize_label_accepts_exact_max_length\n    tests/scripts/bridge/.../test_sanitize_label_rejects_oversize\n\n**The mechanism: `sanitize_label` RAISES `InvalidLabelError` above the ceiling — it does not\ntruncate** (`jira_family/sanitizers.py:56-59`). So a shared 254 does not \"fit\" a long label; it\nmakes the live-validated **Cloud** path refuse a 255-char label that Cloud accepts. That trades a\nsilent DC defect for a loud Cloud regression, and it is precisely the \"don't put the Cloud path at\nrisk\" rule this epic was built around.\n\nREVERTED to 255, with the divergence and this reasoning recorded at the constant. The correct fix is\na **per-deployment ceiling**, following the `comment_max_chars` precedent from\n[rebar:049e-9fac-a821-4ea2] — Cloud keeps 255, DC gets 254. That is a seam change with real blast\nradius, so it wants `file_impact` + `review-plan` before implementation rather than being smuggled\ninto a triage ticket.\n\n**What DID land: the measurement.** A new live cell posts 254- and 255-character labels to the real\ninstance and READS THEM BACK, with 254 as a control leg so a 255 refusal is attributable to the\nceiling rather than to labels being broken or off-screen. Read-back is load-bearing:\naccepted-then-silently-dropped is a third outcome, and scoring it as acceptance is how a silent\nfailure records as a pass — 6afc's shape. Unit pins now use **literal** 255 and 256, because every\npre-existing label test spells that length as `JIRA_LABEL_MAX_CHARS` and therefore checks the\nconstant against itself; if the ceiling ever moves, those tests keep passing while silently no\nlonger testing 255 at all.\n\n### 3. Three unmapped relations — actually FOUR, and both prior accounts were wrong\n\n    rebar.types.Relation  = blocks, depends_on, relates_to, duplicates, supersedes,\n                            discovered_from, caused_by          (SEVEN)\n    RELATION_TO_JIRA_LINK = blocks, depends_on, relates_to      (THREE mapped)\n    unmapped              = duplicates, supersedes, discovered_from, caused_by   (FOUR)\n\nThe pre-fix code comment named `duplicates / supersedes / discovered_from` and omitted `caused_by`.\n**This ticket named `supersedes / discovered_from / caused_by` and omitted `duplicates`.** The set\nhad drifted from the vocabulary in two directions at once — which is what an undocumented decision\nlooks like after a while, and why the remedy is data rather than better prose.\n\n`UNSYNCED_RELATIONS` now names all four with a per-relation reason, and notes that `duplicates` is\nunmapped for a DIFFERENT reason than the other three: stock Jira does have a Duplicate link type,\nbut the measured instance exposes only Blocks and Relates, so mapping it conditionally would make\nbehaviour depend on which link types an instance happens to define. A test asserts the synced and\nunsynced sets **exactly partition** `Relation`, so a newly-added relation with no sync decision\nfails loudly instead of silently never syncing.\n\n### 4. Duplicate `Task` issue types (10003, 10004) — STILL OPEN, deliberately\n\nNot addressed. Resolving types by id is a change to the DC create path, and the alternative\n(documenting the ambiguity as harmless) needs live evidence I do not have yet: specifically whether\nthe provisioned scratch project's issue-type scheme exposes ONE `Task` or both. That is measurable\nin the 3fe5 declared-environment contract, and it is the natural home for AC6's \"assert these so a\nfuture instance change fails loudly\" — but it is a separate measurement, not a doc edit.\n\n**This ticket therefore stays OPEN on ACs 4 and 6.** The label per-deployment ceiling is also\noutstanding, tracked here with the analysis above. Closing now would tick two criteria that are not\nmet."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785864490182261001,"uuid":"7d677df1-db6f-43e1-ad36-a9f5b4121c3d"}